            <ArticleTitle>Impact of diagnosing metabolic syndrome on risk perception.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>522-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5993/AJHB.36.4.9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the effect of using the diagnosis of metabolic syndrome (MetSx) on risk perception.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We randomized 74 participants to receive either the diagnosis of MetSx or the diagnosis of individual cardiovascular risk factors. The MetSx group was subdivided into those receiving written patient education material or written patient education plus educational videos.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At 6 weeks, participants receiving the diagnosis of MetSx were more likely to increase their health concerns, perceived risk of myocardial infarction, and their motivation toward health behavior.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sharing the diagnosis of MetSx increases risk perception and motivation toward a healthier behavior.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
